ssage()`? But that calls getMessage which doesn't mutate the record (only returns str). Actually `record.getMessage()` doesn't set record.message; Formatter.format sets it.\n\nSo plan:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if not keep_full and self.max_message_chars is not None:\n        message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n    # build the record's formatted message without mutating shared fields\n    original_message = record.message if ... \n```\nHmm. Instead of using super().format(record) which relies on record.getMessage(), I could temporarily swap record.message before formatMessage... The standard trick: set `record.message = message` inside a try/finally restoring. But super().format overwrites record.message with getMessage() result.\n\nAlternative: call super().format(record) with the record's args temporarily... no.\n\nTrick used commonly: \n```python\nsaved = record.message\ntry:\n    record.message = truncated\n    # temporarily make getMessage return truncated\n    ...\n```\nMessy.\n\nCleanest: implement format ourselves but reuse Formatter pieces:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)  # sets record.message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        return self._decorate(msg, record)\n    full_message = record.message\n    truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, self.max_message_chars)\n    if truncated != full_message:\n        msg = msg.replace(full_message, truncated, 1)  # only message occurrence\n    return self._decorate(msg, record)\n```\n\nHmm, but exception text could contain the message string too. Using `replace(...,1)` replaces first occurrence, which should be the message since formatMessage output starts with prefix then message. Multiple lines: the prefix is prepended to newlines inside message in the original code — wait, original code does `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` and replaces \"\\n\" with \"\\n\"+prefix. That also modifies inside exception text (fine, keep original behaviour).\n\nThe order matters: original does newline-prefix alignment on the whole msg (including exception) using record.message to find the prefix. If I truncate first, then do newline alignment using the truncated message, that works too.\n\nHmm, but there's a risk: `msg.replace(full_message, truncated, 1)` — the prefix (like `[INFO 2024-...]`) comes before the message. First occurrence of full_message is the message itself unless time prefix coincidentally contains it. Acceptable but hacky.\n\nCleaner robust approach: build formatted output without using record.message mutation:\n\nActually there is a clean approach: temporarily override getMessage on the record? Setting an attribute on the instance `record.getMessage = lambda: truncated`? That mutates the shared record too (adds attribute). But we restore it... Requirement says: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — only these four must not be modified. record.message gets set by Formatter anyway (already mutated by the standard formatter).\n\nHmm, note: `Formatter.format` sets `record.message` and `record.exc_text` on the record. So mutation of record.message is already standard. Requirement only forbids msg, args, exc_info, stack_info.\n\nSo a clean implementation:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    # record.message is now the fully substituted message body\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.message\n        truncated = truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            msg = self._replace_message(msg, message, truncated)\n    ... newline alignment, colour\n```\n\nTo avoid ambiguity with replace, do the replacement while accounting for structure: since `formatMessage` produces the prefix + message (+ newline when message is empty? Actually if the message is empty, formatMessage... logging appends nothing? In Python 3.x, `formatMessage` = self._style.format(record) → uses fmt % record.__dict__, with message empty string. Then format() does `if record.message:` ... the original code guards.\n\nAlternative safest approach: reimplement format building:\n\nInstead of replacing, do:\n```python\nsaved_message = record.message\nrecord.message = truncated\ntry:\n    # rebuild only the message portion\n```\nStill needs rebuild.\n\nI think a robust approach: build the full output ourselves using formatMessage but with a temporary record.__dict__ override for \"message\":\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and not keep_full:\n        message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n    # temporarily present the truncated message to the standard formatter\n    saved = record.__dict__.get(\"message\", _SENTINEL)\n    record.message = message\n    try:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n    finally:\n        ...\n```\nBut super().format calls record.getMessage() and overwrites record.message with the full message. Unless I also override getMessage... Hmm.\n\nActually, wait: does Formatter.format set record.message? Yes line: `record.message = record.getMessage()`.\n\nHmm but the fmt string uses `%(message)s`, which comes from record.__dict__['message']. So if I override getMessage to return truncated, everything works:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = truncated_message\n```\nand fmt uses %(message)s. But Formatter.format overwrites record.message = record.getMessage() before formatMessage.\n\nSo: temporarily set `record.msg`? forbidden.\n\nOK simplest: monkey-patch instance method temporarily:\n\n```python\nget_message = record.getMessage\nrecord.getMessage = lambda: message\ntry:\n    msg = super().format(record)\nfinally:\n    record.getMessage = get_message  # deletes instance attr? using del\n```\nIf getMessage is not an instance attribute originally, `del record.getMessage` restores. Using try/finally with del is safe. But mutating the object shape... acceptable? It's a bit hacky but clean semantics. However `exc_text` caching etc. still fine.\n\nAlternative: don't call super().format at all; replicate:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = ...\n    # temporarily swap in truncated message for the %(message)s substitution\n```\n\nI think a neat approach: use a shallow \"view\" record? E.g., `record.__dict__` copy... too heavy.\n\nActually the replace-based approach has a subtle issue only if prefix duplicates the message. To be safer, I can compute prefix length: the original code finds `msg.find(record.message)`. Message begins right after the prefix in formatMessage output. So:\n\n```python\nfull = record.message\ntruncated = ...\nif truncated != full:\n    idx = msg.find(full)\n    if idx >= 0:\n        msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx+len(full):]\n```\n\nThat's fine and simple. But careful: if full is empty string, find returns 0 and no truncation happens anyway (empty never exceeds).\n\nHmm, but newline alignment: original computes prefix = msg[:msg.find(record.message)]. If I insert truncated then do alignment based on truncated... Let me restructure: do truncation, then compute the alignment using the message actually in msg.\n\nLet me write my own format method:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        message = record.message\n        limit = self.max_message_chars\n        if limit is not None and message and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, limit)\n            if truncated != message:\n                index = msg.find(message)\n                if index >= 0:\n                    msg = msg[:index] + truncated + msg[index + len(message):]\n                    message = truncated\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\nCareful: after truncation, the truncated message contains \"...<omitted>...\" and no \"\\n\" (since we cut middle) but the tail may contain newlines. `msg.find(message)` — message is truncated message, present. OK.\n\nNote: `if \"\\n\" in msg and message` — original used `record.message`. Using local `message` variable equals record.message when not truncated. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: if the message body is huge and appears also in exception? Not our concern.\n\nNow truncation helper:\n\n```python\n_OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, limit: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= limit:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMIT_MARKER\n    remaining = limit - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:limit]  # ? budget >=32 and marker 15 so remaining >=17\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nFor limit=32: remaining = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches spec (开头9、末尾8、标记15 → total 32). \n\nSince limit >= 32 and marker len 15, remaining >= 17 >= 0. Good.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                rais